        {
            "id": "32856579",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "11:30, 14 October 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,717 bytes)",
            "change": 5,
            "summary": "(updating link)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Make mealtimes a regular routine.  Turn off the TV.  Make eating and enjoying the focus.<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Utilize healthy fats.  We tend to think of fat as bad, but that is not always the case.  Many plant-based fats in particular are necessary components of a healthy diet.  Healthy fats are also ideal for weight gain because they provide about nine calories per gram, as opposed to about four per gram for carbohydrates or proteins.<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Have your child drink most of his or her beverage after the meal.  Skip drinking beforehand, and have him or her drink only enough to comfortably (and safely) eat.  This can help keep your child from “filling up” on beverages.<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions can boost nutritious calorie counts in kid-approved foods.  Try, for instance:<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Another helpful article has a recipe for “energy balls,” a treat with dried fruits, nuts, and other goodies that can be stored for long periods and served quickly to hungry kids.<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Make mealtimes a regular routine.  Turn off the TV.  Make eating and enjoying the focus.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Utilize healthy fats.  We tend to think of fat as bad, but that is not always the case.  Many plant-based fats in particular are necessary components of a healthy diet.  Healthy fats are also ideal for weight gain because they provide about nine calories per gram, as opposed to about four per gram for carbohydrates or proteins.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Have your child drink most of his or her beverage after the meal.  Skip drinking beforehand, and have him or her drink only enough to comfortably (and safely) eat.  This can help keep your child from “filling up” on beverages.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions can boost nutritious calorie counts in kid-approved foods.  Try, for instance:<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Another helpful article has a recipe for “energy balls,” a treat with dried fruits, nuts, and other goodies that can be stored for long periods and served quickly to hungry kids.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>"
