                "added": [
                    "*f you are concerned that your child is not gaining weight or is losing weight, talk to your doctor right away, especially if it seems like a big change, or if your child is otherwise ill.",
                    "*Avoid using fattening or sugary foods and drinks – such as chips, cake, candy bars, and sodas – to give your child extra calories. These foods may help increase your child’s weight but can have harmful effects on his or her teeth, metabolism, muscle growth, heart, and brain, and may complicate pre-existing health conditions (such as diabetes)."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*Avoid using fattening or sugary foods and drinks – such as chips, cake, candy bars, and sodas – to give your child extra calories. These foods may help increase your child’s weight but can have harmful effects on his or her teeth, metabolism, muscle growth, heart, and brain, and may complicate pre-existing health conditions (such as diabetes).",
                    "*f you are concerned that your child is not gaining weight or is losing weight, talk to your doctor right away, especially if it seems like a big change, or if your child is otherwise ill."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "25812022",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "20:36, 27 December 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,593 bytes)",
            "change": -243,
            "summary": "(reverted edits after expert review)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Despite the focus on the growing number of overweight children, there are also many kids whose health would benefit from putting on additional weight.  The solution is not as simple as letting underweight kids indulge in their junk food fantasies, though.  Instead, a mixture of changing eating behaviors, choosing nutritious high-calorie foods, and “sneaking” extra calories into meals is best approach to increasing weight in children.  Always consult a medical professional first, however, if you are concerned that your child may be underweight.",
                    "*Avoid using fattening or sugary foods and drinks – such as chips, cake, candy bars, and sodas – to give your child extra calories. These foods may help increase your child’s weight but can have harmful effects on his or her teeth, metabolism, muscle growth, heart, and brain, and may complicate pre-existing health conditions (such as diabetes)."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Despite the focus on the growing number of overweight children, there are also many kids whose health would benefit from putting on additional weight.  The solution is not as simple as letting underweight kids indulge in their junk food fantasies, though.  Instead, a mixture of changing eating behaviors, choosing nutritious high-calorie foods, and “sneaking” extra calories into meals is best approach to increasing weight in children.  Always consult a medical professional first, however, if you are concerned that your child may be underweight. Some kids want to get larger in order to fit into popular clothing brands, like Hollister and American Eagle.",
                    "*Avoid using fattening or sugary foods and drinks – such as chips, cake, candy bars, and sodas – to give your child extra calories. These foods may help increase your child’s weight but can have harmful effects on his or her teeth, metabolism, muscle growth, heart, and brain, and may complicate pre-existing health conditions (such as diabetes). Hearty calorie-filled foods will suffice and work just as well, although eating a fatty dessert once or twice a week will not be bad."
                ]
            }
        },
