            "id": "21162935",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "02:26, 18 October 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(16,004 bytes)",
            "change": 74,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Look for underlying issues.  Some kids, like some adults, are simply naturally slender and have trouble putting on weight.  However, you should try to rule out other reasons for your child's difficulty in gaining weight.<ref>http://pubs.ext.vt.edu/348/348-271/348-271.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Consult your child’s physician.  If your child is receiving regular medical check-ups, the pediatrician may be the one who informs you that your child would benefit from increasing his or her weight.  But never be afraid to raise the subject if you are concerned.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Follow special advice for infants.  Dealing with an infant who needs to gain weight will of course be different from an older child.  While serious causes are rare, normally the issue lies in feeding technique, breast milk production, or gastrointestinal problems.<ref>http://www.babycenter.com/0_failure-to-gain-weight-in-babies_1621.bc?showAll=true</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Feed underweight children more often.  Many times, the problem is not what a child is eating, but simply how much.  Small children have small stomachs and need to eat more frequently than adults.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Make mealtime important.  While sprinkling in snacks as needed, make mealtimes regular focal points in your child’s day.  Teach him or her that eating is both important and enjoyable.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set a good example.  While your kid may need to put on a few pounds, you might benefit from losing a few.  Even if this is the case, your eating habits should not be as different as you may think.  Eating a variety of nutrient-rich foods is essential for the underweight, the overweight, and everyone in between.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 6 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Encourage regular exercise.  Like healthy eating, exercise is more often associated with weight loss than weight gain.  When paired with smart eating, though, it can be part of a weight-gain regimen.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 7 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Skip unhealthy choices.  Yes, cakes, cookies, sodas, and fast food meals have high calorie counts that can increase weight.  However, the cost in other potential health problems (including even childhood diabetes or heart disease) outweighs any small benefits.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 8 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Serve a variety of nutrient-dense foods.  Variety is important not only because it offers the best range of vital nutrients, but also because it helps keep mealtime interesting.  If mealtime is a chore or a bore, it will be more difficult to get your kid to want to eat.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Utilize healthy fats.  We tend to think of fat as bad, but that is not always the case.  Many plant-based fats in particular are necessary components of a healthy diet.  Healthy fats are also ideal for weight gain because they provide about nine calories per gram, as opposed to about four per gram for carbohydrates or proteins.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Select smart snacks.  Children who need to gain weight should be offered regular snacks.  But, as with meals, healthy options should be chosen over empty-calorie foods.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 11 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Watch what and when your child drinks.  Adequate water intake is important for children, but drinking too much can be filling and reduce the amount of food kids eat.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 12 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Make milk your friend.  The ease in adding dairy products like milk and cheese to a wide variety of foods makes them great options for enhancing calorie (and nutrient) content.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 13 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Provide peanut butter.  So long as allergies are not an issue, peanut butter is almost always a welcome addition to a kid’s meal, and provides substantial calories and protein.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 14 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions can boost nutritious calorie counts in kid-approved foods.  Try, for instance:<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Try healthy high-calorie recipes.  The internet if full of recipes suited to increasing weight in children the right way.  For instance, an online pamphlet from the UC-Davis Medical Center (http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf) includes several kid-friendly recipes, such as fruit dip and a “super shake.”[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 16 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Look for underlying issues.  Some kids, like some adults, are simply naturally slender and have trouble putting on weight.  However, you should try to rule out other reasons for your child's difficulty in gaining weight.<ref>http://pubs.ext.vt.edu/348/348-271/348-271.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Consult your child’s physician.  If your child is receiving regular medical check-ups, the pediatrician may be the one who informs you that your child would benefit from increasing his or her weight.  But never be afraid to raise the subject if you are concerned.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Follow special advice for infants.  Dealing with an infant who needs to gain weight will of course be different from an older child.  While serious causes are rare, normally the issue lies in feeding technique, breast milk production, or gastrointestinal problems.<ref>http://www.babycenter.com/0_failure-to-gain-weight-in-babies_1621.bc?showAll=true</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Feed underweight children more often.  Many times, the problem is not what a child is eating, but simply how much.  Small children have small stomachs and need to eat more frequently than adults.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#Make mealtime important.  While sprinkling in snacks as needed, make mealtimes regular focal points in your child’s day.  Teach him or her that eating is both important and enjoyable.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set a good example.  While your kid may need to put on a few pounds, you might benefit from losing a few.  Even if this is the case, your eating habits should not be as different as you may think.  Eating a variety of nutrient-rich foods is essential for the underweight, the overweight, and everyone in between.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Encourage regular exercise.  Like healthy eating, exercise is more often associated with weight loss than weight gain.  When paired with smart eating, though, it can be part of a weight-gain regimen.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Skip unhealthy choices.  Yes, cakes, cookies, sodas, and fast food meals have high calorie counts that can increase weight.  However, the cost in other potential health problems (including even childhood diabetes or heart disease) outweighs any small benefits.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Serve a variety of nutrient-dense foods.  Variety is important not only because it offers the best range of vital nutrients, but also because it helps keep mealtime interesting.  If mealtime is a chore or a bore, it will be more difficult to get your kid to want to eat.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Utilize healthy fats.  We tend to think of fat as bad, but that is not always the case.  Many plant-based fats in particular are necessary components of a healthy diet.  Healthy fats are also ideal for weight gain because they provide about nine calories per gram, as opposed to about four per gram for carbohydrates or proteins.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Select smart snacks.  Children who need to gain weight should be offered regular snacks.  But, as with meals, healthy options should be chosen over empty-calorie foods.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Watch what and when your child drinks.  Adequate water intake is important for children, but drinking too much can be filling and reduce the amount of food kids eat.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Make milk your friend.  The ease in adding dairy products like milk and cheese to a wide variety of foods makes them great options for enhancing calorie (and nutrient) content.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Provide peanut butter.  So long as allergies are not an issue, peanut butter is almost always a welcome addition to a kid’s meal, and provides substantial calories and protein.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions can boost nutritious calorie counts in kid-approved foods.  Try, for instance:<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Try healthy high-calorie recipes.  The internet if full of recipes suited to increasing weight in children the right way.  For instance, an online pamphlet from the UC-Davis Medical Center (http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf) includes several kid-friendly recipes, such as fruit dip and a “super shake.”[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 16.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
