            "id": "18656440",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "13:27, 15 October 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,593 bytes)",
            "change": -488,
            "summary": "(minor revisions & clarifications)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Despite the focus on the growing number of overweight children, there are also many kids whose health would benefit from putting on additional weight.  The solution is not as simple as letting underweight kids indulge in their junk food fantasies, though.  Instead, a mixture of changing eating behaviors, choosing nutritious high-calorie foods, and “sneaking” extra calories into meals is often the best approach to increasing weight in children.  Always consult a medical professional first, however, if you are concerned that your child may be underweight.",
                    "#*Some medications can reduce appetite, so consider this possibility if you child is on medication.",
                    "#Consult your child’s physician.  If your child is receiving regular medical check-ups, the pediatrician may be the one who informs you that your child would benefit from increasing his or her weight.  But never be afraid to raise the subject if you are concerned.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#Follow special advice for infants.  Dealing with an infant who needs to gain weight will of course be different from an older child.  While serious causes are rare, normally the issue lies in feeding technique, breast milk production, or gastrointestinal problems.<ref>http://www.babycenter.com/0_failure-to-gain-weight-in-babies_1621.bc?showAll=true</ref>",
                    "#*Remedies will vary based on your infant's particular situation, but may include:  supplementing breastfeeding with formula (if milk production is insufficient); letting the infant feed as often and for as long as he/she desires (thus avoiding rigid scheduling); changing formula brands (in the case of an intolerance or allergy); or introducing solid foods a bit earlier than the typical age of six months.",
                    "#*Early life weight gain is essential to long-term health, so shortfalls should always be addressed with appropriate medical advice.  Below-average weight gain can almost always be reversed and result in no lasting problems.",
                    "#Feed underweight children more often.  Many times, the problem is not what a child is eating, but simply how much.  Small children have small stomachs and need to eat more frequently than adults.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Children may need to eat five or six smaller meals, along with snacks, each day.",
                    "#Make mealtime important.  While sprinkling in snacks as needed, make mealtimes regular focal points in your child’s day.  Teach him/her that eating is both important and enjoyable.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*If mealtime seems like an annoyance or afterthought, or some sort of punishment (such as sitting until you clean your plate), then children are less likely to be enthusiastic eaters.",
                    "#*Making junk food a rare indulgence will benefit both of you, whether you need to gain or lose weight.",
                    "#Encourage regular exercise.  Like healthy eating, exercise is more often associated with weight loss than weight gain.  When paired with smart eating, though, it can be part of a weight-gain regimen.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#Skip unhealthy choices.  Yes, cakes, cookies, sodas, and fast food meals have high calorie counts that can increase weight.  However, the cost in other potential health problems (including even childhood diabetes or heart disease) outweighs any small benefits.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Calorie-rich but nutrient-poor foods, such as sugary drinks, are not the answer to healthy weight gain.  Foods that are rich in both calories and nutrients are the best option, because they help add weight and provide essential vitamins and minerals.",
                    "#*While fiber is important to a healthy diet, you may not want to overdo it with children trying to gain weight.  Too much whole grain pasta or brown rice can leave a child feeling too full for too long.",
                    "#Utilize healthy fats.  We tend to think of fat as bad, but that is not always the case.  Many plant-based fats in particular are necessary components of a healthy diet.  Healthy fats are also ideal for weight gain because they provide about nine calories per gram, as opposed to about four per gram for carbohydrates or proteins.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Select smart snacks.  Children who need to gain weight should be offered regular snacks.  But, as with meals, healthy options should be chosen over empty-calorie foods.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Focus on high-calorie, high-nutrition, easy to prepare and serve snack options.  For instance, try peanut butter and jelly on whole-grain bread; nuts and dried fruit; apples with cheese; or a turkey wrap with avocado.",
                    "#Watch what and when your child drinks.  Adequate water intake is important for children, but drinking too much can be filling and reduce the amount of food kids eat.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Water is always a good choice, but children who need to gain weight may benefit from drinking whole milk, smoothies or shakes, or even nutritional supplement drinks.  Talk to your child’s pediatrician about the best options.",
                    "#*You can adapt if your child has an allergy or intolerance, or if you prefer not serving milk products.  Soy and almond milks also provide substantial calories and nutrients, and silken tofu can be used in smoothies, for example.",
                    "#*Slather peanut butter on whole grain breads, bananas, apples, celery, multigrain crackers, and pretzels.",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions can boost nutritious calorie counts in kid-approved foods.  Try, for instance:<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#*Serving dried fruit, which children may eat more of because of the lack of water content to fill them up.",
                    "#*Adding cooked beef or chicken to things like pasta, pizza, soup, stew, scrambled eggs, and macaroni and cheese.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>",
                    "#Try healthy high-calorie recipes.  The internet if full of recipes suited to increasing weight in children the right way.  For instance, an online pamphlet from the UC-Davis Medical Center (http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf) includes several kid-friendly recipes, such as fruit dip and a “super shake.”"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "While public focus tends to be on the growing number of overweight children, there are also many kids whose health would benefit from putting on additional weight.  The solution is not as simple as letting underweight kids indulge in their junk food fantasies, though.  Instead, a mixture of changing eating behaviors, choosing healthier foods that are high in calories, and “sneaking” extra calories into meals and snacks is often the best approach to increasing weight in children.  Always consult a medical professional first, however, if you are concerned that your child may be underweight.",
                    "#*Some medications can reduce appetite, so consider this possibility if relevant to your child.",
                    "#Consult your child’s physician.  If your child is receiving regular medical care as recommended, the pediatrician may be the one who informs you that your child would benefit from increasing his or her weight.  But never be afraid to raise the subject if you are concerned.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#Follow special advice for infants.  Dealing with an infant who needs to gain weight will of course be different from an older child.  While serious causes can rarely be at play, often the issue lies in feeding technique, breast milk production, or gastrointestinal problems.<ref>http://www.babycenter.com/0_failure-to-gain-weight-in-babies_1621.bc?showAll=true</ref>",
                    "#*Remedies will vary based on your infant's particular situation, but may include adjustments such as:  supplementing breastfeeding with formula (if milk production is insufficient); letting the infant feed as often and for as long as he/she desires (thus avoiding rigid scheduling); changing formula brands (in the case of an intolerance or allergy); or introducing solid foods a bit earlier than the typical age of six months.",
                    "#*Early life weight gain is essential to long-term health, so shortfalls should always be addressed with appropriate medical advice.  Below-average weight gain can almost always be reversed and thereby result in no lasting problems, however.",
                    "#Feed underweight children more often.  Many times, the problem is not what a child is eating, but simply how much.  Small children in particular have small stomachs and need to eat more frequently than adults.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Children may need to eat five or six smaller meals, or even three larger and three smaller meals, as well as snacks each day.",
                    "#Make mealtime important.  While sprinkling in snacks as needed, make mealtimes regular focal points in your child’s day (as well as yours).  Teach them that eating is both important and enjoyable.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*If mealtime seems like an annoyance or afterthought, or some sort of punishment — think of sitting until your plate is cleaned — then children are less likely to be enthusiastic eaters.",
                    "#*Making junk food a rare indulgence will benefit both of you, regardless of your weight circumstances.",
                    "#Encourage regular exercise.  Like healthy eating, exercise is more often associated with weight loss than weight gain.  It is beneficial for everyone, though, and when paired with smart eating, can be part of a weight-gain regimen.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#Skip unhealthy choices.  Yes, cakes, cookies, sodas, fast food meals, and the like have high calorie counts and can help increase weight.  However, the cost in other potential health problems, including even diabetes or heart disease in children, outweighs any small benefits.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Calorie-rich but nutrient-poor foods — sugary drinks are an excellent example — are not the answer to healthy weight gain.  Rather, foods that are rich in both calories and nutrients are the best option, because they help add weight and provide essential vitamins and minerals.",
                    "#*While fiber is important to a healthy diet, you may not want to overdo it with children trying to gain weight.  Too much whole grain pasta or brown rice, for instance, can leave a child feeling too full for too long.",
                    "#Utilize healthy fats.  We have been conditioned to think of all fats as bad, but that is not the case.  Many plant-based fats in particular are necessary components of a healthy diet.  Healthy fats are also ideal for weight gain because they provide about nine calories per gram, as opposed to about four per gram for carbohydrates or proteins.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Select smart snacks.  Children who need to gain weight should be offered regular snacks, but, as with meals, healthy options should be chosen over empty-calorie foods.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*High-calorie, high-nutrition, easy to prepare and serve snack options include peanut butter and jelly on whole-grain bread; nuts and dried fruit; apples with cheese; or a turkey wrap with avocado.",
                    "#Watch what and when your child drinks.  Adequate water intake is as important for children as adults, but drinking too much can be filling and reduce the amount of food kids eat.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Water is always sensible, but children who need to gain weight may benefit from drinking whole milk, smoothies or shakes, or even nutritional supplement drinks.  Talk to your child’s pediatrician about the best options.",
                    "#*Obviously, if your child has an allergy or intolerance, or if you prefer not serving milk products, you’ll have to adapt.  Soy and almond milks also provide substantial calories and nutrients, and silken tofu, for instance, can be used in smoothies.",
                    "#*Slather peanut butter on whole grain breads, bananas, apples, celery, multigrain crackers, and pretzels, among many other options.",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions, along with those already mentioned, can boost calorie counts in kid-approved foods in a nutritious manner.  Try, for instance:<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#*Serving dried fruit, which children may want to eat more of because of the lack of water content to fill them up.",
                    "#*Add cooked beef or chicken — finely ground or chopped if masking them is important for the child — to things like pasta, pizza, soup, stew, scrambled eggs, and macaroni and cheese.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>",
                    "#Try healthy high-calorie recipes.  The internet if full of recipes of all sorts, including those suited to increasing weight in children the right way.  For instance, an online pamphlet from the UC-Davis Medical Center (available at http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf) includes several kid-friendly recipes, such as fruit dip and a “super shake.”"
                ]
            }
