            "summary": "(total revision, new steps & sources)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "While public focus tends to be on the growing number of overweight children, there are also many kids whose health would benefit from putting on additional weight.  The solution is not as simple as letting underweight kids indulge in their junk food fantasies, though.  Instead, a mixture of changing eating behaviors, choosing healthier foods that are high in calories, and “sneaking” extra calories into meals and snacks is often the best approach to increasing weight in children.  Always consult a medical professional first, however, if you are concerned that your child may be underweight.",
                    "",
                    "==Steps==",
                    "===Addressing Behaviors===",
                    "#Consult your child’s physician.  If your child is receiving regular medical care as recommended, the pediatrician may be the one who informs you that your child would benefit from increasing his or her weight.  But never be afraid to raise the subject if you are concerned.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Food intolerances or allergies, digestive problems, and a range of other medical issues can sometimes be the cause of a child being underweight, and physicians can help diagnose and treat such problems.",
                    "#*That said, most often the issue can be improved by changes that you and your child can make at home.  But the advice of a medical professional is always beneficial.",
                    "#Feed underweight children more often.  Many times, the problem is not what a child is eating, but simply how much.  Small children in particular have small stomachs and need to eat more frequently than adults.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Children may need to eat five or six smaller meals, or even three larger and three smaller meals, as well as snacks each day.",
                    "#*Whenever an underweight child feels hungry, feed him or her.",
                    "#Make mealtime important.  While sprinkling in snacks as needed, make mealtimes regular focal points in your child’s day (as well as yours).  Teach them that eating is both important and enjoyable.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*If mealtime seems like an annoyance or afterthought, or some sort of punishment — think of sitting until your plate is cleaned — then children are less likely to be enthusiastic eaters.",
                    "#*Make mealtimes a regular routine.  Turn off the TV.  Make eating and enjoying the focus.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Set a good example.  While your kid may need to put on a few pounds, you might benefit from losing a few.  Even if this is the case, your eating habits should not be as different as you may think.  Eating a variety of nutrient-rich foods is essential for the underweight, the overweight, and everyone in between.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Children learn by watching you.  If you regularly try new foods and make healthy options, like fruits, vegetables, and whole grains your first choices, they are more likely to adopt these habits.",
                    "#*Making junk food a rare indulgence will benefit both of you, regardless of your weight circumstances.",
                    "#Encourage regular exercise.  Like healthy eating, exercise is more often associated with weight loss than weight gain.  It is beneficial for everyone, though, and when paired with smart eating, can be part of a weight-gain regimen.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*For older children in particular, adding muscle mass is likely to increase weight, and is always healthier than adding body fat.",
                    "#*Exercise can often stimulate the appetite, so try encouraging physical activity before mealtimes and see if that helps.",
                    "",
                    "===Choosing Calorie- and Nutrient-Dense Foods===",
                    "#Skip unhealthy choices.  Yes, cakes, cookies, sodas, fast food meals, and the like have high calorie counts and can help increase weight.  However, the cost in other potential health problems, including even diabetes or heart disease in children, outweighs any small benefits.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Calorie-rich but nutrient-poor foods — sugary drinks are an excellent example — are not the answer to healthy weight can.  Rather, foods that are rich in both calories and nutrients are the best option, because they help add weight and provide essential vitamins and minerals.",
                    "#*Don’t tell your child he or she needs to “fatten up” or “get some meat on those bones” — say that you both need to choose and eat more healthy foods.",
                    "#Serve a variety of nutrient-dense foods.  Variety is important not only because it offers the best range of vital nutrients, but also because it helps keep mealtime interesting.  If mealtime is a chore or a bore, it will be more difficult to get your kid to want to eat.<ref>http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*A high-calorie, high-nutrient diet for weight gain in children should include starchy carbohydrates (pastas, breads, cereals); at least five servings of fruits and vegetables daily; proteins (meat, fish, eggs, beans); and dairy products (milk, cheese, etc.).",
                    "#*All children under two should consume full-fat dairy products, and your child’s doctor may recommend continuing this practice past that age to support weight gain.",
                    "#*While fiber is important to a healthy diet, you may not want to overdo it with children trying to gain weight.  Too much whole grain pasta or brown rice, for instance, can leave a child feeling too full for too long.",
                    "#Select smart snacks.  Children who need to gain weight should be offered regular snacks, but, as with meals, healthy options should be chosen over empty-calorie foods.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*High-calorie, high-nutrition, easy to prepare and serve snack options include peanut butter and jelly on whole-grain bread; nuts and dried fruit; apples with cheese; or a turkey wrap with avocado.",
                    "#*For treats, present options like bran muffins, granola bars, and yogurt before resorting to cakes, cookies, and ice cream.",
                    "#Watch what and when your child drinks.  Adequate water intake is as important for children as adults, but drinking too much can be filling and reduce the amount of food kids eat.<ref>http://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/nutrition-and-healthy-eating/expert-answers/underweight/faq-20058429</ref>",
                    "#*Empty-calorie drinks like soda provide no nutritional value, while the amount of sugar in fruit juices can be bad for teeth and overall health when consumed in excess.",
                    "#*Water is always sensible, but children who need to gain weight may benefit from drinking whole milk, smoothies or shakes, or even nutritional supplement drinks.  Talk to your child’s pediatrician about the best options.",
                    "#*Have your child drink most of his or her beverage after the meal.  Skip drinking beforehand, and have him/her drink only enough to comfortably (and safely) eat.  This can help keep your child from “filling up” on beverages.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "",
                    "===Enhancing Calorie Counts in Foods===",
                    "#Make milk your friend.  The ease in adding dairy products like milk and cheese to a wide variety of foods makes them great options for enhancing calorie (and nutrient) content.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>",
                    "#*Smoothies and milkshakes are easy ways to help kids drink their calories, and the addition of fresh fruit can further boost the nutrition content.",
                    "#*Cheese can be melted into or sprinkled on top of just about anything, from eggs to salads to steamed veggies.",
                    "#*Try adding milk to canned soups instead of water, and serve sour cream, cream cheese, or yogurt-based dips with fruits or veggies.",
                    "#*Obviously, if your child has an allergy or intolerance, or if you prefer not serving milk products, you’ll have to adapt.  Soy and almond milks also provide substantial calories and nutrients, and silken tofu, for instance, can be used in smoothies.",
                    "#Provide peanut butter.  So long as allergies are not an issue, peanut butter is almost always a welcome addition to a kid’s meal, and provides substantial calories and protein.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>",
                    "#*Slather peanut butter on whole grain breads, bananas, apples, celery, multigrain crackers, and pretzels, among many other options.",
                    "#*You can also blend it into smoothies and shakes, and use it as a layer of “glue” between two pancakes or French toast squares.",
                    "#*If peanut allergies are an issue, almond butter can be a good alternative.  Flaxseeds and flaxseed oil also provide ample calories and nutrients.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#Take small steps to add calories.  Simple additions and substitutions, along with those already mentioned, can boost calorie counts in kid-approved foods in a nutritious manner.  Try, for instance:<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "#*Cooking pasta and rice in chicken broth instead of water.",
                    "#*Serving dried fruit, which children may want to eat more of because of the lack of water content to fill them up.",
                    "#*Adding flaxseed oil, with its mild flavor, to everything from salad dressings to peanut butter and banana smoothies.",
                    "#*Add cooked beef or chicken — finely ground or chopped if masking them is important for the child — to things like pasta, pizza, soup, stew, scrambled eggs, and macaroni and cheese.<ref>http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf</ref>",
                    "#Try healthy high-calorie recipes.  The internet if full of recipes of all sorts, including those suited to increasing weight in children the right way.  For instance, an online pamphlet from the UC-Davis Medical Center (available at http://www.ucdmc.ucdavis.edu/cancer/pedresource/pedres_docs/HowHelpChildGainWeight.pdf) includes several kid-friendly recipes, such as fruit dip and a “super shake.”",
                    "#*It also explains how to make high-calorie milk by adding two tablespoons of dry powdered milk to each cup of whole or lowfat milk.",
                    "#*Another helpful article has a recipe for “energy balls,” a treat with dried fruits, nuts, and other goodies that can be stored for long periods and served quickly to hungry kids.<ref>http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/on-parenting/post/underweight-child-heres-how-to-put-on-a-few-healthy-pounds/2012/09/24/42a5ecdc-0691-11e2-a10c-fa5a255a9258_blog.html</ref>",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "",
                    "__METHODS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Although childhood is often a time of skinny legs and growth spurts, there are some circumstances in which children may need to gain weight. Follow these suggestions to help your child gain weight for medical or health reasons.",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Offer Healthy High-Calorie Beverages ===",
                    "For young toddlers and preschoolers who are underweight, milk-based toddler formulas, high-calorie fortified drinks, and standard beverages can provide simple sources of energy and can speed up weight gain. These drinks should not be substituted for food if your child needs to gain weight, and instead should be served with meals or as drinks between meals.",
                    "#Buy PediaSure. Fortified beverages targeted at helping young children recoup growth in height and weight may help your child add the necessary calories and nutrients to his or her diet, particularly if he or she is a picky eater.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Serve whole or 2% milk. If your child is significantly underweight, encourage extra fat and calories through the consumption of healthy beverages such as milk. Only serving non-fat or low-fat dairy may reduce your child’s opportunity to consume sufficient calories from his or her diet.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Serve Energy-Dense Foods ===",
                    "Serving healthy foods that are either high in calories or high in nutrients (preferably both) will help your child gain weight by providing the energy and nutrients his or her body needs to build muscle or store fat.",
                    "#Serve healthy foods. To help your child gain weight the healthiest way possible, try to serve foods that are both full of nutrients and relatively high in calories. It is best to avoid foods that are high in added sugars, saturated fats, and sodium.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Do not limit nutrient-dense foods. Nutrient-dense foods have a high amount of nutrients (like vitamins, minerals, and fiber) compared to their calories.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Offering higher quantities of healthy, nutrient-dense foods such as fresh fruits and vegetables and lean protein, can help your child get excellent nutrition, while still working toward a higher daily calorie count.",
                    "#*Kid-friendly examples of nutrient-dense foods include strawberries and chicken breast, or a bowl of full-fat milk with rice and honey.",
                    "#Provide regular energy-dense food sources. Energy-dense foods have high calorie counts compared to the amount of other nutrients they provide or the size of the serving.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Energy-dense foods can contribute significantly to healthy weight gain for your child, but should be consumed in moderation. For instance, avoid serving pasta for all three meals or making every snack a bagel or sunflower seeds.",
                    "#*Healthy energy-dense foods include nuts and seeds, bread and bagels, pastas, avocados, healthy oils (like olive oil or canola oil for cooking), dried fruits, and 100% fruit juices.",
                    "=== Encourage Your Child to Have Dips, Spreads, and Condiments ===",
                    "Additional calories in kid-friendly dips, spreads, and condiments can help add flavor and healthy energy to your child’s meals. These toppings are often high in calories, making a meal or snack more energy-dense without your child feeling like he or she is consuming considerably more food.",
                    "#Offer dips or cheese with fruit and vegetables. Provide a few tablespoons of peanut butter when you serve apples or celery, offer dressing when you provide sliced vegetables, or serve snacks, sandwiches, and side dishes with cheese.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Encourage reasonable use of butter and other spreads. Many spreads are energy-dense and add flavors that kids enjoy, such as butter, fruit jam, peanut butter, Nutella, cream cheese, and other flavored spreads. The flavor may encourage your child to eat more food, and the calorie-dense spread will help him or her gain weight.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Allow condiments or toppings on meals and desserts. Encourage your child to customize the flavor of his or her meal or dessert by adding favorite condiments, such as mayonnaise, ketchup, sour cream, ranch dressing, and other popular toppings. Provided your child does not go overboard (eating the condiment as much as the actual food), using condiments can be an effective way to add relatively healthy calories to his or her daily diet.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Encourage Frequent Snacking ===",
                    "Regular snacking provides calories between meals and may supply your child with the opportunity to eat more throughout the day than if food is only offered at three daily meals. Breakfast is essential for a healthy weight, and providing regular mealtimes daily will help keep frequent snacking from interfering with mealtime appetite.",
                    "#Pack a morning and afternoon snack for school. Preparing snacks ahead of time and making sure your child eats them at some point during the day will help your child consume more calories. If necessary, secure permission for your child to eat the snacks between classes or to sip on a high-calorie, healthy drink if eating is not allowed.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Offer an after-school snack. Offering even a small snack after school and before dinner can add essential calories for weight gain.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Try a half-sandwich, a fruit and cheese plate, a baked potato with cheese and salsa, a cheese quesadilla, or another kid-friendly snack.",
                    "#*Oatmeal raisin cookies with full-fat milk can be an appealing, relatively healthy and energy-dense snack for your child.",
                    "#Do not allow children to skip meals. Breakfast is an excellent opportunity to provide plenty of essential nutrients and energy at the beginning of the day and can help your child achieve a healthy weight.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Offer fruits, cereals, toast or bagels with cream cheese, a peanut butter sandwich, and 100% juice or milk to provide a range of nutrients and a healthy amount of calories to your child.",
                    "#*If the cost of food is a barrier for your family, consider enrolling your child in a meal-assistance program where he or she can receive free or discounted meals at school.",
                    "#Offer dessert. If your child is still hungry after having eaten a meal, offer dessert. Consider fresh fruit with pound cake, vanilla ice cream with nuts and berries, a frozen banana dipped in chocolate or smeared with peanut butter, a few chunks of chocolate with dried fruit, or a couple of cookies with milk.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "=== Consult a Health Professional ===",
                    "Physical and mental health problems may lead to serious weight loss in children. If you are having difficulty increasing your child’s weight or if you suspect a medical condition may be causing your child’s underweight problem, consult a health professional. A medical professional may be able to diagnose a specific barrier to weight gain or may be able to offer tailored solutions for weight gain for your child.",
                    "#Rule out a health condition. Sometimes underweight is due to a simple lack of sleep or significant physical activity on behalf of the child, but other times there are specific health problems that may be preventing weight gain in your child.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Some physical conditions, such as Cystic Fibrosis or prematurity, can cause poor nutrient absorption and difficulty gaining weight despite a healthy appetite and sufficient calorie intake.",
                    "#*Mental health issues such as intense stress, grief, or an eating disorder may cause a child to be underweight or to lose weight unexpectedly.",
                    "#Consult a pediatric dietitian. A nutritionist can review your child’s daily dietary and physical activity in detail and identify any weaknesses in the diet as well as potential areas for improvement. Many dietitians can offer specific suggestions for increasing your child’s weight and may be available for consultation on an ongoing basis until your child’s weight issues are resolved.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Discuss any special dietary rules you follow. If your family follows a vegan, vegetarian, or other highly restrictive diet, your child may not be getting enough calories or nutrients to gain weight adequately. You may need to consider altering his or her diet or taking guided extra efforts to ensure that he or she is consuming enough calories, protein, fat, and other necessary nutrients.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "* Make sure that you're child doesn't skip meals at school(trust me-this happens a lot when they just want to play at school). Encourage them to have a heavy breakfast, reasonable lunch, filling dinner. also include 3 glasses of milk in their everyday lives and some healthy snacks.eg) stringers.",
                    "*Encouraging your child to participate in strength training or muscle-building activities can help him or her add healthy muscle weight.",
                    "*http://pediasure.com/kid-nutrition-products",
                    "*http://www.choosemyplate.gov/steps/nutrientdensefoodchoices.pdf",
                    "*http://www.nhs.uk/Livewell/Goodfood/Pages/Underweightyoungchild.aspx",
                    "*http://www.mayoclinic.com/health/underweight/AN00597"
                ]
            }
        },
