            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Buy PediaSure. Fortified beverages targeted at helping young children recoup growth in height and weight may help your child add the necessary calories and nutrients to his or her diet, particularly if he or she is a picky eater.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Serve whole or 2% milk. If your child is significantly underweight, encourage extra fat and calories through the consumption of healthy beverages such as milk. Only serving non-fat or low-fat dairy may reduce your child’s opportunity to consume sufficient calories from his or her diet.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "Serving healthy foods that are either high in calories or high in nutrients (preferably both) will help your child gain weight by providing the energy and nutrients his or her body needs to build muscle or store fat.",
                    "#Serve healthy foods. To help your child gain weight the healthiest way possible, try to serve foods that are both full of nutrients and relatively high in calories. It is best to avoid foods that are high in added sugars, saturated fats, and sodium.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Do not limit nutrient-dense foods. Nutrient-dense foods have a high amount of nutrients (like vitamins, minerals, and fiber) compared to their calories.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Provide regular energy-dense food sources. Energy-dense foods have high calorie counts compared to the amount of other nutrients they provide or the size of the serving.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Offer dips or cheese with fruit and vegetables. Provide a few tablespoons of peanut butter when you serve apples or celery, offer dressing when you provide sliced vegetables, or serve snacks, sandwiches, and side dishes with cheese.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Encourage reasonable use of butter and other spreads. Many spreads are energy-dense and add flavors that kids enjoy, such as butter, fruit jam, peanut butter, Nutella, cream cheese, and other flavored spreads. The flavor may encourage your child to eat more food, and the calorie-dense spread will help him or her gain weight.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Allow condiments or toppings on meals and desserts. Encourage your child to customize the flavor of his or her meal or dessert by adding favorite condiments, such as mayonnaise, ketchup, sour cream, ranch dressing, and other popular toppings. Provided your child does not go overboard (eating the condiment as much as the actual food), using condiments can be an effective way to add relatively healthy calories to his or her daily diet.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "Regular snacking provides calories between meals and may supply your child with the opportunity to eat more throughout the day than if food is only offered at three daily meals. Breakfast is essential for a healthy weight, and providing regular mealtimes daily will help keep frequent snacking from interfering with mealtime appetite.",
                    "#Pack a morning and afternoon snack for school. Preparing snacks ahead of time and making sure your child eats them at some point during the day will help your child consume more calories. If necessary, secure permission for your child to eat the snacks between classes or to sip on a high-calorie, healthy drink if eating is not allowed.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Offer an after-school snack. Offering even a small snack after school and before dinner can add essential calories for weight gain.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Do not allow children to skip meals. Breakfast is an excellent opportunity to provide plenty of essential nutrients and energy at the beginning of the day and can help your child achieve a healthy weight.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Offer dessert. If your child is still hungry after having eaten a meal, offer dessert. Consider fresh fruit with pound cake, vanilla ice cream with nuts and berries, a frozen banana dipped in chocolate or smeared with peanut butter, a few chunks of chocolate with dried fruit, or a couple of cookies with milk.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Rule out a health condition. Sometimes underweight is due to a simple lack of sleep or significant physical activity on behalf of the child, but other times there are specific health problems that may be preventing weight gain in your child.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Consult a pediatric dietitian. A nutritionist can review your child’s daily dietary and physical activity in detail and identify any weaknesses in the diet as well as potential areas for improvement. Many dietitians can offer specific suggestions for increasing your child’s weight and may be available for consultation on an ongoing basis until your child’s weight issues are resolved.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Discuss any special dietary rules you follow. If your family follows a vegan, vegetarian, or other highly restrictive diet, your child may not be getting enough calories or nutrients to gain weight adequately. You may need to consider altering his or her diet or taking guided extra efforts to ensure that he or she is consuming enough calories, protein, fat, and other necessary nutrients.[[Image:Increase Weight in Children Step 15.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
