                    "#Allow condiments or toppings on meals and desserts. Encourage your child to customize the flavor of his or her meal or dessert by adding favorite condiments, such as mayonnaise, ketchup, sour cream, ranch dressing, and other popular toppings. Provided your child does not go overboard (eating the condiment as much as the actual food), using condiments can be an effective way to add relatively healthy calories to his or her daily diet.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "Regular snacking provides calories between meals and may supply your child with the opportunity to eat more throughout the day than if food is only offered at three daily meals. Breakfast is essential for a healthy weight, and providing regular mealtimes daily will help keep frequent snacking from interfering with mealtime appetite.",
                    "#Pack a morning and afternoon snack for school. Preparing snacks ahead of time and making sure your child eats them at some point during the day will help your child consume more calories. If necessary, secure permission for your child to eat the snacks between classes or to sip on a high-calorie, healthy drink if eating is not allowed.",
                    "#Offer an after-school snack. Offering even a small snack after school and before dinner can add essential calories for weight gain.",
                    "#Do not allow children to skip meals. Breakfast is an excellent opportunity to provide plenty of essential nutrients and energy at the beginning of the day and can help your child achieve a healthy weight.",
                    "#Offer dessert. If your child is still hungry after having eaten a meal, offer dessert. Consider fresh fruit with pound cake, vanilla ice cream with nuts and berries, a frozen banana dipped in chocolate or smeared with peanut butter, a few chunks of chocolate with dried fruit, or a couple of cookies with milk.",
                    "#Rule out a health condition. Sometimes underweight is due to a simple lack of sleep or significant physical activity on behalf of the child, but other times there are specific health problems that may be preventing weight gain in your child.",
                    "#Consult a pediatric dietitian. A nutritionist can review your child’s daily dietary and physical activity in detail and identify any weaknesses in the diet as well as potential areas for improvement. Many dietitians can offer specific suggestions for increasing your child’s weight and may be available for consultation on an ongoing basis until your child’s weight issues are resolved.",
                    "#Discuss any special dietary rules you follow. If your family follows a vegan, vegetarian, or other highly restrictive diet, your child may not be getting enough calories or nutrients to gain weight adequately. You may need to consider altering his or her diet or taking guided extra efforts to ensure that he or she is consuming enough calories, protein, fat, and other necessary nutrients."
                ]
            }
        },
