            "id": "11052272",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "06:06, 9 September 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,490 bytes)",
            "change": 2,
            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos; enlarging intro image)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "",
                    "#Buy PediaSure. Fortified beverages targeted at helping young children recoup growth in height and weight may help your child add the necessary calories and nutrients to his or her diet, particularly if he or she is a picky eater.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Serve whole or 2% milk. If your child is significantly underweight, encourage extra fat and calories through the consumption of healthy beverages such as milk. Only serving non-fat or low-fat dairy may reduce your child’s opportunity to consume sufficient calories from his or her diet.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Serve healthy foods. To help your child gain weight the healthiest way possible, try to serve foods that are both full of nutrients and relatively high in calories. It is best to avoid foods that are high in added sugars, saturated fats, and sodium.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Do not limit nutrient-dense foods. Nutrient-dense foods have a high amount of nutrients (like vitamins, minerals, and fiber) compared to their calories.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Provide regular energy-dense food sources. Energy-dense foods have high calorie counts compared to the amount of other nutrients they provide or the size of the serving.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Offer dips or cheese with fruit and vegetables. Provide a few tablespoons of peanut butter when you serve apples or celery, offer dressing when you provide sliced vegetables, or serve snacks, sandwiches, and side dishes with cheese.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Encourage reasonable use of butter and other spreads. Many spreads are energy-dense and add flavors that kids enjoy, such as butter, fruit jam, peanut butter, Nutella, cream cheese, and other flavored spreads. The flavor may encourage your child to eat more food, and the calorie-dense spread will help him or her gain weight.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Allow condiments or toppings on meals and desserts. Encourage your child to customize the flavor of his or her meal or dessert by adding favorite condiments, such as mayonnaise, ketchup, sour cream, ranch dressing, and other popular toppings. Provided your child does not go overboard (eating the condiment as much as the actual food), using condiments can be an effective way to add relatively healthy calories to his or her daily diet.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Buy PediaSure. Fortified beverages targeted at helping young children recoup growth in height and weight may help your child add the necessary calories and nutrients to his or her diet, particularly if he or she is a picky eater.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 1.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Serve whole or 2% milk. If your child is significantly underweight, encourage extra fat and calories through the consumption of healthy beverages such as milk. Only serving non-fat or low-fat dairy may reduce your child’s opportunity to consume sufficient calories from his or her diet.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 2.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Serve healthy foods. To help your child gain weight the healthiest way possible, try to serve foods that are both full of nutrients and relatively high in calories. It is best to avoid foods that are high in added sugars, saturated fats, and sodium.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 3.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Do not limit nutrient-dense foods. Nutrient-dense foods have a high amount of nutrients (like vitamins, minerals, and fiber) compared to their calories.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 4.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Provide regular energy-dense food sources. Energy-dense foods have high calorie counts compared to the amount of other nutrients they provide or the size of the serving.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 5.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Offer dips or cheese with fruit and vegetables. Provide a few tablespoons of peanut butter when you serve apples or celery, offer dressing when you provide sliced vegetables, or serve snacks, sandwiches, and side dishes with cheese.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 6.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Encourage reasonable use of butter and other spreads. Many spreads are energy-dense and add flavors that kids enjoy, such as butter, fruit jam, peanut butter, Nutella, cream cheese, and other flavored spreads. The flavor may encourage your child to eat more food, and the calorie-dense spread will help him or her gain weight.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 7.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Allow condiments or toppings on meals and desserts. Encourage your child to customize the flavor of his or her meal or dessert by adding favorite condiments, such as mayonnaise, ketchup, sour cream, ranch dressing, and other popular toppings. Provided your child does not go overboard (eating the condiment as much as the actual food), using condiments can be an effective way to add relatively healthy calories to his or her daily diet.<br><br>[[Image:1796816 8.jpg|center|416px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
